    <glyph id="ROS_TRANSCRIPTIONAL_RESPONSE" class="process" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="ROS-responsive transcription" />
      <bbox x="281.86" y="545.36" w="210.98" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="ROS_TRANSCRIPTIONAL_RESPONSE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="best-evidenced node" />
        <bbox x="287.86" y="536.36" w="133.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="ROS_TRANSCRIPTIONAL_RESPONSE__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="worst discriminator" />
        <bbox x="287.86" y="536.36" w="133.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:ROS_TRANSCRIPTIONAL_RESPONSE" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="verified">
          <qbo:quantumClass>structural_context</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Parmagnani2022" doi="10.3390/biom12121824" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Transcriptomics and metabolomics of ROS modulation in near-null magnetic field-induced Arabidopsis: field reduction alters ROS-associated transcriptional and metabolic programmes.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Zsigmond2008" doi="10.1104/pp.107.111260" direction="altered">Arabidopsis PPR40 connects abiotic stress responses to mitochondrial electron transport, establishing a route from respiratory state to stress signalling.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>This entity carries demonstrated near-null-field evidence but NO proposed quantum route, and is classed structural_context to say so explicitly. It is the amplifier's output: threshold-like signalling turns an upstream change too small to measure directly into a transcriptional response. That sensitivity is what makes it the best-evidenced node in the atlas and simultaneously a poor discriminator — it reports that something moved upstream, never what.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
